# LangGraph Patterns (TypeScript)

**Trigger**: Active when creating or modifying LangGraph agent/workflow files in `src/lib/agent/`.

> **Note**: LangGraph.js is aspirational — not yet implemented in this project. These patterns serve as the reference for when the agent layer is built.

> **Extended reference**: See the `langgraph-orchestration` skill for full workflow examples (signal analysis, cross-signal inference, article generation), streaming, subgraphs, and performance patterns.

## State Design

Use TypeScript interfaces with explicit field types. Every field that accumulates across nodes needs a reducer pattern. Message fields use LangGraph's built-in message handling.

### Reducer Selection

| Pattern | Implementation | Use Case |
|---------|----------------|----------|
| Replace value | Direct assignment | `sentiment`, `confidenceScore`, single-result fields |
| Append list | Spread operator or concat | `facts`, `patterns`, accumulated results |
| Messages | Built-in message reducer | Chat history (built-in reducer) |

### State Field Rules

- **Keep state minimal**: Only store values that persist across nodes or routers
- **No transient data in state**: Pass temporary values through function scope
- **Type all fields**: Use concrete types, never `any`
- **Always provide defaults via reducers**: Append reducers start from `[]`; replace fields are set by nodes

```typescript
// src/lib/agent/types.ts
import { BaseMessage } from "@langchain/core/messages";

interface SignalAnalysisState {
  signalId: string;
  rawText: string;
  facts: Array<{ text: string; confidence: number }>;
  sentiment: "POSITIVE" | "NEGATIVE" | "NEUTRAL";
  confidenceScore: number;
  error?: string;
  messages: BaseMessage[];
}
```

